<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trave</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Trave is a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, which flows past Bad Segeberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> at Travemünde. Lübeck was refounded in 1159 on a hill between the Trave and the Wakenitz, and the river gave the city the access to the sea that made it the leading port of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trave.jpg" alt="Trave" width="320" height="172" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bryan Rutherford · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elbe%E2%80%93L%C3%BCbeck_Canal.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Trave is a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>, about 124 kilometres long, running from its source near the village of Gießelrade in Ostholstein to Travemünde, where it flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>. It rises and flows south-west through the Wardersee to Bad Segeberg, then south to Bad Oldesloe, where it bends eastward and passes Moisling, a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>, before reaching Lübeck.</p><p>Its tributaries include the Wakenitz and the Stepenitz. Sea-going vessels can navigate it from the Baltic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> ports. The lower course arose in the last ice age, the Weichselian glaciation, when glacier flows carved deep fjords into what is now the Baltic coast; its lower end is called the Traveförde.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the upper Trave, together with the Schwentine, formed part of the Limes Saxoniae and the western boundary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wagria">Wagria</a>. In medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a> the river was reshaped for shipping and for the town's defence, turning the Old Town into an island. South of Lübeck Cathedral the channel forks, the original channel being the Town Trave, while the town moat branches off to the west and the Canal Trave runs in the former bed of the Wakenitz. The river forms part of the medieval fortifications. North of Lübeck, on the peninsula between the Schwartau and the Trave, lies the site of the Slavic castle of Liubice.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>North of the Old Town the seaports begin, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lubeck">Lübeck</a>'s commercial port lies between the Old Town and Teerhof Island. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>–Lübeck Canal joins the river from the south shortly before Lübeck. The Herren Tunnel and numerous bridges cross the Trave, and a ferry connects Travemünde with Priwall. The lower reaches form the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</a>. The surrounding area is a protected habitat under the European Union Habitats Directive, with several nature preserves, and the German Friends of Nature named the Trave its German Riverscape of the Year for 2016/17.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trave” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Henry the Lion refounded Lübeck on the <b>Trave</b> in 1159 as a German port to the Baltic.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-lubeck">Free and Hanseatic City of Lübeck</a></li></ul></section>
